from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q061-120')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Mathematics',topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(81,'Complex Analysis',r'''γ(t)=e^{2πit}, 0≤t≤1, and I=∫_γ eᶻe^{1/z}dz. Which are true?''',[r'I=0.',r'I/(2πi)∈{4n:n∈ℤ,n≥1}.',r'I=2πiΣₙ≥₀1/n!.',r'I=2πiΣₙ≥₀1/[n!(n+1)!].'],'D',['The integral is 2πi times the coefficient of z⁻¹.','In the product of the two exponential series, choose indices satisfying k−m=−1.'],r'''Expand eᶻe^{1/z}=Σₖ,ₘ≥₀z^{k−m}/(k!m!). The z⁻¹ coefficient uses m=k+1, giving R=Σₖ≥₀1/[k!(k+1)!]. Both series converge absolutely and uniformly on the unit circle, legitimizing multiplication and integration. Thus I=2πiR, proving D. R>0, refuting A. Also 1<R<e<4, since each term is ≤1/k! with strict inequality for k≥1; hence R is neither e (C) nor a positive multiple of 4 (B).''',r'''For a positively oriented simple closed contour enclosing an isolated singularity, the residue theorem says that the integral of a holomorphic integrand away from that singularity equals 2πi times its residue. The residue is the coefficient of z⁻¹ in a Laurent expansion about the singularity, even when the singularity is essential. Exponential series converge absolutely and uniformly on compact sets, and on a circle avoiding zero both eᶻ and e^{1/z} have such convergent expansions. Their product coefficient is obtained by matching exponent differences, not by selecting merely one term from each series. Absolute convergence permits rearrangement of the resulting double series. Positivity and comparison with the exponential series help distinguish alternative proposed values without requiring a decimal approximation.''',[('Evaluate ∮_|z|=1 eᶻ/z dz.','The residue is the constant coefficient of eᶻ, namely 1. The positively oriented integral is 2πi.'),('Find the residue of e^{1/z} at zero.','Its Laurent expansion is Σₙ≥₀z^{−n}/n!. The z⁻¹ coefficient is 1, so the residue is 1 despite the essential singularity.')])
add(82,'Complex Analysis',r'''f is a bijective holomorphic map of the punctured unit disk D×={0<|z|<1} onto itself. Which hold?''',[r'lim_z→0 f(z) does not exist.',r'The limit exists and has modulus ≤1.',r'lim_z→0 f(z)=0.',r'f(z)=e^{iθ}z for some θ∈ℝ.'],'BCD',['Boundedness makes the puncture removable for f and its inverse.','The extensions must fix zero; apply Schwarz’s lemma in both directions.'],r'''Since |f|<1, f extends holomorphically across zero to F. The maximum modulus principle gives |F(0)|<1, since F is nonconstant. If F(0)=a≠0, the holomorphic inverse g on D× is defined at a, and g(f(z))=z implies g(a)=0, impossible because g maps into D×. Thus F(0)=0, proving B,C. The inverse likewise extends and fixes zero. Schwarz’s lemma gives |F(z)|≤|z| and |G(w)|≤|w|; using G(F(z))=z forces equality. The equality case yields F(z)=e^{iθ}z, proving D and refuting A.''',r'''A bounded holomorphic function near an isolated puncture has a removable singularity by Riemann's removable singularity theorem. An injective holomorphic map has nonzero derivative and a holomorphic inverse onto its image, permitting the same argument for an automorphism's inverse. Schwarz's lemma states that a holomorphic self-map of the unit disk fixing zero satisfies |f(z)|≤|z|, and equality at any nonzero point forces a rotation. Applying it to a map and its inverse proves that a disk automorphism fixing zero must be a rotation. For punctured-disk automorphisms, one must first show the removable extension fixes the missing point; merely knowing the limit lies in the closed disk is not enough. The maximum modulus principle excludes a boundary value for a nonconstant extension.''',[('Which automorphisms of the unit disk fix zero?','Schwarz’s lemma for the map and inverse forces modulus equality, hence they are precisely rotations z↦e^{iθ}z.'),('Can f(z)=z² be a punctured-disk automorphism?','It maps the punctured disk onto itself but is not injective, since z and −z have the same image. Bijectivity is essential.')])
add(83,'Abstract Algebra',r'''G acts on left cosets T={gH:g∈G} by π(g)(g₁H)=gg₁H. For prime p, in which cases is ker π trivial?''',[r'G=GL₂(𝔽_p), |H|=p.',r'G=SL₂(𝔽_p), |H|=p.',r'p≡3 mod4, G=GL₂(𝔽_p)/SL₂(𝔽_p), |H|=2.',r'p≡1 mod4, G=GL₂(𝔽_p)/SL₂(𝔽_p), |H|=2.'],'AB',['The kernel is the core ⋂g gHg⁻¹, a normal subgroup contained in H.','Order-p subgroups of GL₂ and SL₂ are Sylow subgroups; the determinant quotient is abelian.'],r'''ker π=⋂_{g∈G}gHg⁻¹. Since |H|=p, the core is either trivial or H; it equals H only if H is normal. In both GL₂(𝔽_p) and SL₂(𝔽_p), the highest p-power dividing the group order is p. All order-p subgroups are Sylow and conjugate to U={[[1,a],[0,1]]:a∈𝔽_p}. Conjugation by [[0,−1],[1,0]]∈SL₂ changes U into the distinct lower-unipotent subgroup, so U, hence H, is not normal. Thus A,B have trivial core. The determinant gives GL₂/SL₂≅𝔽_p×, an abelian group; every subgroup H is normal and core=H, nontrivial for |H|=2. Neither C nor D holds.''',r'''The action of a group on cosets is a permutation representation. An element acts trivially on every coset exactly when it lies in every conjugate of the stabilizer subgroup, so the kernel is the core, the largest normal subgroup of G contained in H. A prime-order subgroup has no intermediate nontrivial subgroups, making triviality of its core equivalent to nonnormality. Sylow's theorems identify and conjugate subgroups whose orders are maximal prime powers dividing a finite group order. Determinant maps GL₂ over a field onto its nonzero multiplicative group, with kernel SL₂; the first isomorphism theorem identifies the quotient. Abelian groups have every subgroup normal, so a coset action with nontrivial stabilizer cannot be faithful there. The congruence class of p does not override this abelian quotient argument.''',[('For G=S₃ and H generated by a transposition, is the coset action faithful?','H has order 2 and is not normal. Its core, being a proper subgroup of H, is trivial, so the action is faithful.'),('For G=C₆ and H its order-two subgroup, find the coset-action kernel.','G is abelian, so every conjugate equals H. The core and kernel are H, of order 2; the action is not faithful.')])
add(84,'Abstract Algebra','Which statements about group automorphisms are true?',['Aut(G₁)≅Aut(G₂) implies G₁≅G₂.','If |G|=2, Aut(G×G) is abelian.','Aut(ℂ,+) is abelian.','If G is finite, Aut(G) is finite.'],'D',['Automorphisms of a finite group are a subset of its permutations.','Test the first assertion with trivial and order-two groups; test the second with GL₂(𝔽₂).'],r'''D holds because Aut(G) embeds into the finite permutation group of G. A fails: the trivial group and C₂ both have trivial automorphism groups but are not isomorphic. If G=C₂ then G×G is the two-dimensional 𝔽₂ vector space, with automorphism group GL₂(𝔽₂)≅S₃, nonabelian; B fails. For C, multiplication M(z)=iz and conjugation C(z)=z̄ are additive group automorphisms, but C(M(z))=−iz̄ and M(C(z))=iz̄, so they do not commute. Thus only D is true.''',r'''An automorphism is a bijective homomorphism from a group to itself, and all automorphisms form a group under composition. For a finite group with n elements this group is contained in S_n and hence finite. Isomorphic original groups have isomorphic automorphism groups, but the converse is false: the invariant does not recover the entire group. An elementary abelian p-group is a vector space over 𝔽_p, and its group automorphisms are exactly its invertible linear transformations, giving GL_n(𝔽_p). For an additive complex group, additive automorphisms include real-linear maps such as conjugation and multiplication by nonzero complex numbers; they are not restricted to complex-linear scalar maps. To disprove abelianness, exhibit two explicit automorphisms whose compositions differ on a point.''',[('Compute Aut(C₃).','An automorphism sends a generator to either of the two generators. Thus Aut(C₃) has order 2 and is isomorphic to C₂.'),('Give noncommuting invertible maps on 𝔽₂².','Take S=[[0,1],[1,0]] and T=[[1,1],[0,1]]. Multiplication gives ST=[[0,1],[1,1]] and TS=[[1,1],[1,0]], which differ.')])
add(85,'Abstract Algebra',r'''G₁,G₂≤G. Which are true?''',[r'If G₁◁G, (G₂G₁)/G₁≅G₂/(G₂∩G₁).',r'If Hᵢ◁Gᵢ, (G₁×G₂)/(H₁×H₂)≅(G₁/H₁)×(G₂/H₂).',r'If G₁◁G₂ and G₂◁G, then G₁◁G.',r'Every subgroup of prime index is normal.'],'AB',['Apply the first isomorphism theorem to the natural quotient homomorphisms.','Normality is not transitive; index two is special, but an arbitrary prime index is not.'],r'''A follows from the map G₂→(G₂G₁)/G₁, g↦gG₁. It is onto with kernel G₂∩G₁. B follows from the surjective map (g₁,g₂)↦(g₁H₁,g₂H₂), whose kernel is H₁×H₂. C fails for G=S₄,G₂=V₄ (the identity and three double transpositions), and G₁ generated by one double transposition: G₁ is normal in abelian V₄ and V₄ is normal in S₄, but conjugation in S₄ permutes the three order-two subgroups. D fails for an order-two subgroup of S₃, of prime index 3 but not normal.''',r'''The first isomorphism theorem identifies a group modulo a homomorphism's kernel with its image. The second isomorphism theorem is a standard application: for N normal in G and H a subgroup, HN/N≅H/(H∩N). Product quotients are handled by applying the quotient maps componentwise; the kernel is the product of the respective kernels. Normality means invariance under conjugation by every element of the ambient group, so normality in a subgroup need not extend to a larger ambient group even if that subgroup is itself normal. Subgroups of index two are always normal because there are only two cosets, but prime indices greater than two do not ensure this. Always specify the ambient group for each normality assertion.''',[('Show 2ℤ/6ℤ≅ℤ/3ℤ.','Map 2k to k modulo 3; it is onto and has kernel 6ℤ, so the first isomorphism theorem gives the result.'),('Is the subgroup {e,(12)} normal in S₃?','No: conjugation by (123) sends (12) to (23). Its index is 3, showing that prime index alone is insufficient.')])
add(86,'Abstract Algebra',r'''f:ℚ[X]→ℚ[X] is a ring homomorphism with f(1)=1, and fⁿ is its n-fold composition. Which hold?''',['If f is onto, every fⁿ is onto.',r'ker fⁿ⁺¹=ker fⁿ for some n≥1.','If f is onto, f is one-to-one.','If f is one-to-one, f is onto.'],'ABC',['A unital homomorphism fixes every rational and is determined by h=f(X).','If h is nonconstant, substitution is injective; if h is constant, f is idempotent.'],r'''f fixes ℚ and has f(P)=P(h) for h=f(X). If h is nonconstant, deg P(h)=deg P·deg h for nonzero nonconstant P, so no nonzero polynomial maps to zero; f and all its powers are injective, giving identical zero kernels. If h=c∈ℚ, f is evaluation at c and f²=f, so the kernels also stabilize at n=1. This proves B. A follows by composing surjections. An onto f cannot have constant h; hence h is nonconstant and f injective, proving C (indeed onto requires degree h=1). D fails for h=X²: substitution is injective, but every image has only even powers and X is absent.''',r'''A unital endomorphism of a polynomial ring over ℚ fixes the rational constants: additivity fixes integers and multiplicativity preserves their inverses. The universal property of a polynomial ring therefore says it is completely determined by the image h of X, with action P↦P(h). Composition of nonconstant polynomials multiplies degrees, so substitution by a nonconstant polynomial is injective. Surjectivity requires h of degree one, since an image equal to X forces degree P times degree h to equal one. Constant substitution is an evaluation homomorphism with principal kernel (X−c) and is idempotent. More generally polynomial rings over fields are Noetherian and ascending chains of ideals stabilize, explaining eventual kernel stabilization of endomorphism iterates. Injectivity and surjectivity of an infinite set map are distinct properties.''',[('Is P(X)↦P(3X+2) an automorphism of ℚ[X]?','Yes. Its inverse substitutes (X−2)/3, so it is bijective.'),('Find the kernels of all positive powers of evaluation at X=4.','Evaluation is idempotent because rational outputs are fixed. Every positive power has kernel (X−4).')])
add(87,'Abstract Algebra',r'''f(X)=X⁵+X+1 and g(X)=X⁵−X+1 in ℚ[X]. Which are true?''',['f is irreducible but g is not.','g is irreducible but f is not.','Both are irreducible.','Neither is irreducible.'],'B',['Factor f using the cyclotomic factor X²+X+1.','Reduce g modulo 3 and exclude linear and irreducible quadratic divisors.'],r'''f=(X²+X+1)(X³−X²+1), so it is reducible. Modulo 3, g=X⁵+2X+1 has value 1 at each of 0,1,2, so it has no linear factor. The monic irreducible quadratics over 𝔽₃ are X²+1, X²+X+2, X²+2X+2. Division of g gives remainders 1, X+1, X+1 respectively, none zero. A reducible degree-five polynomial must have an irreducible factor of degree at most two; otherwise every factor degree is at least three and their sum exceeds five. Thus g is irreducible modulo 3. As g is monic with integer coefficients, Gauss’s lemma and reduction prove irreducibility over ℚ. Only B holds.''',r'''A polynomial over a field is irreducible when it cannot be factored into two positive-degree polynomials. For a primitive integer polynomial, Gauss's lemma equates reducibility over ℚ with reducibility over ℤ. If its leading coefficient survives reduction modulo a prime and the reduced polynomial is irreducible, the original is irreducible over ℚ. Degree determines what factors must be checked: a reducible degree-five polynomial has a factor of degree one or two, while excluding rational roots alone does not suffice. Over a small finite field one can list all monic irreducible quadratics and compute division remainders. A successful explicit factorization immediately proves reducibility. The modular test is one-directional: reducibility modulo a particular prime does not imply reducibility over ℚ.''',[('Prove X³+X+1 is irreducible over ℚ.','Modulo 2 it has no roots: values at 0 and 1 are both 1. A cubic over a field is reducible iff it has a root, so the reduction is irreducible; Gauss’s lemma proves the claim.'),('Why does no rational root not prove irreducibility for a quartic?','(X²+1)(X²+2)=X⁴+3X²+2 has no real, hence no rational, root but has a nontrivial quadratic factorization.')])
add(88,'Abstract Algebra',r'''p>2 is prime and 𝔽̄_p is an algebraic closure. Which hold?''',[r'For f∈𝔽_p[X] and a root α, 𝔽_p(α) is its splitting field.',r'For irreducible f,g of the same degree and a root α of f, 𝔽_p(α) is a splitting field of g.',r'𝔽_p[X] has infinitely many irreducible polynomials.',r'{a+b:a,b∈𝔽_p}⊆{a²+b²:a,b∈𝔽_p}.'],'BCD',['Finite fields of a given size are unique inside an algebraic closure.','The set of squares has (p+1)/2 elements, so it meets its translate reflected about any target.'],r'''A is false: choose a nonsquare d∈𝔽_p and f=X(X²−d), with root α=0. Then 𝔽_p(α)=𝔽_p lacks the other roots. For B, irreducible degree d gives 𝔽_p(α)=𝔽_{pᵈ}; every irreducible polynomial of degree d splits in this unique field, and its roots generate it. C follows by Euclid's argument: if r₁,…,r_k were all monic irreducibles, the nonconstant polynomial Xr₁⋯r_k+1 would have an irreducible divisor not among them. For D, let Q be the square set, of size (p+1)/2. For any c∈𝔽_p, Q and c−Q have total size p+1 and must intersect. Thus c=a²+b². The left set is all 𝔽_p, so D holds.''',r'''Finite fields of size pᵈ exist for every positive integer d and are unique as subfields of an algebraic closure, consisting of roots of X^{pᵈ}−X. An irreducible degree-d polynomial over 𝔽_p has its roots in 𝔽_{pᵈ}, with the Frobenius automorphism cycling them, and any root generates that field. For a general reducible polynomial, a single root need not generate a field containing all other roots. Polynomial rings over fields have infinitely many monic irreducibles by a product-plus-one argument analogous to Euclid's prime proof. In an odd finite field, every nonzero square has exactly two square roots, giving (p+1)/2 squares including zero. A pigeonhole intersection then represents every field element as a sum of two squares, regardless of whether it is itself a square.''',[('Represent every element of 𝔽₃ as a sum of two squares.','Squares are 0 and 1. We have 0=0+0,1=1+0,2=1+1, covering all elements.'),('If α is a root of an irreducible cubic over 𝔽₅, what is |𝔽₅(α)|?','The extension degree is 3, so the field has 5³=125 elements and contains all roots of every irreducible cubic over 𝔽₅.')])
add(89,'Topology','p:ℝ→ℝ is a nonconstant polynomial. Which are true?',['Preimages of compact sets under p are compact.','Preimages of connected sets under p are connected.','At every point p restricts on some open neighborhood to a homeomorphism onto an open set.','Images of bounded sets under p are bounded.'],'AD',['Nonconstant polynomials have |p(x)|→∞ as |x|→∞.','Use p(x)=x² for the connectedness and local-homeomorphism claims.'],r'''A compact K is closed and bounded. Continuity makes p⁻¹(K) closed, and |p(x)|→∞ ensures it is bounded. By Heine–Borel it is compact, proving A. If E is bounded it lies in [−M,M], and continuity bounds p on that compact interval, proving D. B fails for p=x² and K={1}: the preimage is {−1,1}, disconnected though K is connected. C fails for x² at 0, since every open neighborhood contains ±ε with the same image, so no restriction there is injective or a homeomorphism.''',r'''A continuous map sends compact sets to compact sets, but its preimages of compact sets need not generally be compact. A map with compact preimages is called proper; a nonconstant real polynomial is proper because its absolute value tends to infinity at both ends of the real line. In ℝ, Heine–Borel identifies compactness with being closed and bounded. Connected subsets of ℝ are intervals, but continuity preserves connectedness under images rather than arbitrary preimages. A local homeomorphism must be injective on a neighborhood and map that neighborhood onto an open set. Critical turning points of polynomials can prevent this property. To bound an image of a bounded nonclosed set, enclose the set in a compact interval and use continuity there.''',[('Find p⁻¹([1,4]) for p(x)=x² and test compactness.','It is [−2,−1]∪[1,2], closed and bounded, hence compact, but disconnected.'),('Is x³ a local homeomorphism at zero despite derivative zero?','Yes. It is globally strictly increasing, bijective, and has continuous inverse cube root. A nonzero derivative is sufficient, not necessary, for a local homeomorphism.')])
add(90,'Topology',r'''S={a+b√2:a,b∈ℚ}⊂ℝ has the subspace topology. Which are true?''',['S is dense in ℝ.',r'S\ℚ is dense in ℝ.',r'S\ℚ is discrete in the subspace topology on S.','S is connected.'],'AB',['S contains ℚ, and S\ℚ contains √2+ℚ.','Both are dense, while S is countable and contains more than one point.'],r'''S contains ℚ, so its closure is ℝ, proving A. Every √2+q with q∈ℚ is irrational and lies in S; translating a dense set preserves density, so B holds. C is false: for any s∈S\ℚ, the points s+1/n also belong to S\ℚ, are distinct from s, and converge to it, so no point is isolated. S is countable because ℚ² is countable. A connected subset of ℝ with two different points must contain the interval between them, which is uncountable. Since S includes 0 and 1 but is countable, it is not connected; D fails.''',r'''A subset of ℝ is dense if every nonempty open interval contains one of its points. Supersets and translates of dense sets are dense, so rational subsets often provide easy density proofs. A discrete subspace is one where each point is isolated relative to that subspace; arbitrarily close distinct points or a convergent sequence within the set disproves discreteness. Connected subsets of the real line are exactly intervals, including singleton intervals. A countable subset containing at least two points therefore cannot be connected, because the interval joining those points is uncountable. Density does not imply connectedness: ℚ itself is the standard counterexample. In a rational span involving an irrational number, a nonzero irrational coefficient ensures irrationality, and adding a rational number preserves it.''',[('Is √3+ℚ dense in ℝ and discrete?','It is a translate of ℚ, hence dense. For any point s, s+1/n stays in the set and converges to s, so it is not discrete.'),('Is ℚ connected in its subspace topology?','No. Splitting at an irrational number such as √2 gives two nonempty relatively open sets ℚ∩(−∞,√2) and ℚ∩(√2,∞) that separate it.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
